# Exercise: Greatest Common Divisor
#
# `gcd` finds the greatest common divisor of two integers using
# the Euclidean algorithm.
#
# Examples:
#   12 8 gcd     # Stack: ( 4 )
#   15 10 gcd    # Stack: ( 5 )
#   7 13 gcd     # Stack: ( 1 ) — coprime
#
# Your task: *compose* `gcd` with integer division to simplify a
# fraction. Unlike the earlier exercises in this chapter (which
# mostly wrapped a single std:imath word), `simplify-numerator`
# is not a rename of `gcd` — it builds on top of it:
#
#   1. compute gcd(numerator, denominator)
#   2. divide the numerator by that gcd
#   3. return the result (the simplified numerator)
#
# So for 12/8: gcd(12, 8) = 4, and 12 / 4 = 3.
#
# Note: integer division `i./` returns ( quotient Bool ) — the
# Bool reports success/failure (false on divide-by-zero). For
# this exercise the divisor is always non-zero, so `drop` the
# flag and keep the quotient.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

include std:imath

# I AM NOT DONE

: simplify-numerator ( Int Int -- Int )
    # Given numerator and denominator, return simplified numerator.
    # Hint: `gcd` consumes both inputs, so use `over over` to peek
    # at the pair before calling it, then arrange the stack so you
    # can divide the numerator by the gcd via `i./` (and drop the
    # success flag it leaves on top).
    # Your code here
    drop
;

# Do not edit below this line

: test-gcd ( -- )
    48 18 gcd
    6 test.assert-eq
;

: test-simplify ( -- )
    # gcd(12, 8) = 4, so 12 / 4 = 3 → simplified numerator is 3
    12 8 simplify-numerator
    3 test.assert-eq
;
